WEBVTT

00:00.000 --> 00:12.000
Move the water. Keep bulk nitrate, temperature, thallus area and uptake capacity the same.
Blue tracers show water movement; gold parcels add illustrative random motion.

00:12.000 --> 00:19.000
Water velocity and nitrate concentration are different profiles.
Water slows towards the stationary surface; water velocity is zero at the surface.

00:19.000 --> 00:26.000
Zero water velocity does not stop molecular motion.
Diffusion continues to supply nitrate towards the surface.

00:26.000 --> 00:35.000
Uptake depletes nitrate as water passes downstream.
The moving line selects position in a steady concentration field, not elapsed time.

00:35.000 --> 00:46.000
Stronger flow keeps the depleted region closer to the surface.
Advection acts along the thallus; molecular diffusion supplies the surface.

00:46.000 --> 00:55.000
At the same downstream position, surface concentration rises from 0.42 to 1.00 micromoles N per litre.
Uptake flux increases from 0.0344 to 0.0669 micromoles N per square metre per second.

00:55.000 --> 01:04.000
The concentration difference becomes smaller, but the surface gradient becomes steeper.
Uptake increases by 94.2 percent, with the same diffusion coefficient and uptake capacity.

01:04.000 --> 01:10.000
Now lower uptake capacity in both treatments.
This is a sequence of steady comparisons, not the time course of a physiological change.

01:10.000 --> 01:20.000
Surface nitrate rises from 8.11 to 9.04 micromoles N per litre with stronger flow.
Uptake now increases by only 2.1 percent: capacity is already restrictive.

01:20.000 --> 01:27.000
Stronger flow, almost unchanged uptake. What might be restricting the rate now?
Keep bulk concentration, temperature and tissue state fixed.

01:27.000 --> 01:36.000
Membrane transport or internal processing may restrict uptake.
A small flow response is consistent with that explanation, but does not identify the transport mechanism.
